WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.273
A producer is a type that only hands values of T out.

2
00:00:06.273 --> 00:00:09.239
A feed of payments, an iterator, a Supplier.

3
00:00:09.239 --> 00:00:11.531
You call it, and it gives you a T.

4
00:00:12.233 --> 00:00:14.457
Here is a source of card payments.

5
00:00:14.457 --> 00:00:15.831
It holds two of them.

6
00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:17.470
And here is totalCents.

7
00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.531
It wants a source of any Payment.

8
00:00:19.531 --> 00:00:23.029
It calls next until it gets null, and adds up the cents.

9
00:00:23.233 --> 00:00:25.867
Can you hand it your source of card payments?

10
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:29.028
Run the substitution test from lesson 3 of this track.

11
00:00:29.233 --> 00:00:33.416
The only thing totalCents ever does with the source is call next.

12
00:00:33.416 --> 00:00:35.668
Each call hands back a CardPayment.

13
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.085
Every CardPayment is a Payment, so the sum works.

14
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.083
Nothing can go wrong, because nothing ever goes into a source.

15
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.641
But the compiler can't see that by itself.

16
00:00:46.641 --> 00:00:50.100
Generic types are invariant unless you say otherwise.

17
00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:53.625
So this call fails, with an argument type mismatch.

18
00:00:53.833 --> 00:00:58.188
In Kotlin you say otherwise once, on the declaration, with out.

19
00:00:58.188 --> 00:01:00.055
Now the same call compiles.

20
00:01:00.867 --> 00:01:02.249
This is covariance.

21
00:01:02.249 --> 00:01:04.869
CardPayment is a subtype of Payment.

22
00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:10.760
Because Source is declared out, a Source of CardPayment is a subtype of a Source of Payment.

23
00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:15.923
The arrow between the elements carries over to the sources, in the same direction.

24
00:01:16.733 --> 00:01:18.475
Java has no out keyword.

25
00:01:18.475 --> 00:01:24.064
Each Java method that takes a source says it at the use site, with ? extends.

26
00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:26.926
Kotlin's out is declaration-site variance.

27
00:01:26.926 --> 00:01:30.979
You write it once, on Source, and every caller gets it for free.

28
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.953
Java's ? extends is use-site variance.

29
00:01:33.953 --> 00:01:38.083
A method that forgets it is stricter than it needs to be.

30
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:42.107
That is the promise out makes: T only comes out.

31
00:01:42.333 --> 00:01:48.740
Add a method that takes a T in, and the compiler rejects the out, and tells you which position broke it.
